Opus encoder: runtime ceiling on the coded audio band (P13), for a
microphone that is a Bluetooth hands-free link, without touching the fixed
60 ms / 32 kbps CBR configuration.

New process-wide knob in the P8 tuning API, read by the P5 encoder code:
- `qaudion::SetOpusEncoderMaxPlaybackRate(hz)` / `OpusEncoderMaxPlaybackRate()`
  (rtc_base/qaudion_tuning.{h,cc}): -1 (the default) = no app limit; 8000..48000
  = cap the coded band at that playback rate; any other value is ignored (the
  previous value is kept), never RTC_CHECKed. Bumps `TuningGeneration()` only
  when the stored value changes, like the other setters. Android:
  `PeerConnectionFactory.setQaudionOpusEncoderMaxPlaybackRate(int)`; ObjC:
  `+[RTCPeerConnectionFactory setQaudionOpusEncoderMaxPlaybackRate:]` (NSInteger
  clamped just outside the valid range, so an out-of-range value is ignored and
  never turned into a valid cap).
- `audio_encoder_opus.{cc,h}`: the effective rate is
  `min(SDP maxplaybackrate, app limit)`, handed to the existing
  `WebRtcOpus_SetMaxPlaybackRate()` (its bucket mapping, <= 8000 narrowband,
  <= 16000 wideband, ..., is what turns it into OPUS_SET_MAX_BANDWIDTH, a
  ceiling that libopus may stay under, not a forced bandwidth). The SDP value
  stays a hard ceiling: the app can only lower it. It is applied in
  `RecreateEncoderInstance()` - moved from where upstream sets it to after the
  `TuningGeneration()` read, for the same reason P5 reads the generation first
  - and re-applied in `EncodeImpl()`'s existing one-atomic-load generation
  poll, so a change in the middle of a call is picked up on the next frame.
- Logs (numbers only): the existing `Q-AUDION opus-enc complexity=.. min_loss_pct=..`
  line gets ` max_rate_hz=..`; and, only while an app limit is active, one
  `Q-AUDION opus-enc tx_mode=.. tx_bw=.. bytes=.. max_rate_hz=..` line each time
  the mode/band read from the packet's TOC byte changes (mode 0 SILK, 1 hybrid,
  2 CELT; band 0 narrowband, 1 mediumband, 2 wideband, 3 super-wideband, 4
  fullband), at most 16 per encoder instance. This is the field confirmation
  that the limit reached the bitstream.

Not touched, on purpose: bitrate, frame size / ptime, CBR, in-band FEC, DTX,
channel count. Nothing here calls WebRtcOpus_SetBitRate, _EnableCbr, _SetFec,
_SetDtx or changes `config_`. Packet size and packet rate cannot change: with
OPUS_SET_VBR(0) at 32 kbps and 60 ms every packet is 240 bytes in every
mode/band (checked on libopus itself, see "Measured").

Why a hook is needed at all: libopus (float build, Chromium's third_party/opus
at upstream 55513e81, no FIXED_POINT or DISABLE_FLOAT_API in its BUILD.gn)
runs its signal analysis, and with it bandwidth detection, only at complexity
>= 7 (opus_encoder.c: `if (st->silk_mode.complexity >= 7 && st->Fs>=16000)`).
The app's complexity tiers 10/8 are above that, 6/5 are below. And the
detection has a floor that depends on the rate: for `equiv_rate <= 30000` the
detected band is never reported below wideband (`min_detected_bandwidth`), and
at 32 kbps CBR 60 ms with a 10 % loss floor equiv_rate is about 24-29 kbps. So
libopus never goes to narrowband by itself in this configuration, goes to
wideband only when the analysis is on and agrees, and with the analysis off
(tiers 6/5) codes hybrid super-wideband/fullband whatever the input.

Measured (standalone harness against libopus built from that exact upstream
commit, float, VOIP, 60 ms, 32 kbps CBR, in-band FEC on, planned loss 10 %,
~59 s of synthetic speech per voice, inputs band-limited like a CVSD link
(300-3400 Hz at 8 kHz) and like an mSBC link (16 kHz), then upsampled to
48 kHz; ITU P.862 PESQ with the 8 kHz or 16 kHz reference, no packet loss
applied, classic PLC):
- Packet size: 240 bytes for every packet in every mode/band, with and
  without the cap.
- Band chosen without a cap, narrowband input: complexity 10/8: wideband
  SILK for ~99 % of the packets; complexity 6/5: hybrid super-wideband or
  fullband for ~99 %. Never narrowband.
- With a cap (narrowband for the CVSD-like input): always SILK-only at the
  capped band; the encoder CPU time drops by about 40 % at complexity 10
  (0.8 ms against 1.4 ms per 60 ms frame on the build server) and by about
  20 % at complexity 5.
- Quality: capping is NOT a clear gain. PESQ-NB, narrowband input, two voices:
  complexity 10: 3.12 / 3.10 without a cap, 3.22 / 2.99 with the narrowband
  cap; complexity 5: 4.44 / 4.32 without a cap (hybrid), 3.17 / 2.89 with
  it. The same input scores 4.5 with in-band FEC off in the four
  configurations tried (no cap and wideband cap, complexity 10 and 5), and
  also with FEC on and the planned loss at 0 %: what lowers the score is
  SILK-only mode with FEC on and a non-zero planned loss (4.53 at 0 %, 3.36 at
  3 %, 3.12 at 10 %, 2.34 at 20 %), which hybrid mode does not show. So at
  the policy's 32 kbps / 10 % floor a cap forces SILK-only mode and the
  clean-path score drops to the level the analysis-on tiers already have;
  with real packet loss the SILK-only mode has its LBRR to recover frames,
  and this harness (classic PLC, no deep PLC/OSCE of the real decoder) cannot
  say how the two compare on a lossy link. The cap gives no measured gain on
  the clean path and a loss at the complexity tiers that run hybrid today, so
  the apps are expected to use this hook behind a switch that stays off until
  a field comparison says otherwise.

Order: last of the audio series, after P9 (m150/series). It touches only
files P8 (qaudion_tuning.{h,cc}, PeerConnectionFactory.java,
peer_connection_factory.cc, RTCPeerConnectionFactory.{h,mm}) and P5
(audio_encoder_opus.{cc,h}) already touch, and no other patch of the series
touches any of them. No new GN target, no new dependency.

Touches (dir "."): rtc_base/qaudion_tuning.h, rtc_base/qaudion_tuning.cc,
modules/audio_coding/codecs/opus/audio_encoder_opus.cc,
modules/audio_coding/codecs/opus/audio_encoder_opus.h,
sdk/android/api/org/webrtc/PeerConnectionFactory.java,
sdk/android/src/jni/pc/peer_connection_factory.cc,
sdk/objc/api/peerconnection/RTCPeerConnectionFactory.h,
sdk/objc/api/peerconnection/RTCPeerConnectionFactory.mm.

Tests (not part of the series, added by m150/ci/apply-tests.py): T6 gets three
tests for the new setter (domain, ignored values, generation); T12
(m150/tests/audio_encoder_opus_qaudion_tests.inc, spliced into
audio_encoder_opus_unittest.cc) drives the real AudioEncoderOpusImpl with the
wire policy: a cap set before the call is narrowband from the first packet, a
wideband cap never codes above wideband, the SDP limit stays a hard ceiling,
a cap set and lifted in the middle of a call takes hold and releases (libopus
needs seconds for it: its SILK layer changes its internal rate one step at a
time on frames without speech), and every packet is 240 bytes throughout.

Verified: `git apply --check` and a real apply are clean against a
reconstruction of the pinned tree for the eight files above: the raw files of
webrtc-sdk/webrtc @ ba469aa2093ba950066258ca0a59a6fbd1295582 with P8 and P5
applied in series order (the other patches of the series do not touch these
files). The scenarios of the T12 tests were replayed on libopus itself (same
upstream commit, same encoder settings, same test signal): narrowband from
the first packet with a cap at start, wideband never above wideband, and a
mid-call cap reaching narrowband after about 100 packets and leaving it within
7 after the lift (hybrid again after about 190).

Not verified: nothing here was compiled or run through GN/clang (no
toolchain on the author's machine; the build workflows do it) - the C++, the
JNI/ObjC glue and the gtest additions are read-checked only. The first run of
test-m150-patches on this branch is the compile check. The behaviour on a real
Bluetooth route and on arm64 (NEON paths of the same code) is not measured.

What remains for a shippable binary (not done here): dispatch
build-m150-android and build-m150-ios (and the Windows build if the desktop
engine should carry it) from this branch with a new release_suffix, then
point the apps at the new artifacts (the Android AAR in libs/, the iOS
WebRTC binaryTarget url and checksum in Package.swift). The apps call the new
setter through a lookup that does nothing on a binary without it.

